  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The dimensions of a cubic water tank are $2.4\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.4\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.4\\,\\text{m},$  where each dimension is measured to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ Rounded to the nearest tenth of a cubic meter, what is the smallest possible volume of the tank?",
  "worked_solution": "Each dimension is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{m}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *smallest* possible volume of the tank. Therefore, we require the *lower bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe lower bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2.4\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m}=2.35\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 2.4\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m}=2.35\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 2.4\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m}=2.35\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible volume of the tank is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2.35\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.35\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.35\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =12.977\\,875\\,{\\text{m}}^{3} \\\\  & ≈13.0\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth of a cubic meter.",
